Matching and the algebra
description: types compose as predicates; consumers carry their own acceptance formula
- >
  Types are predicates, so JSON Schema's boolean combinators are the whole algebra: `allOf` is
  conjunction, `anyOf` disjunction, `not` negation, and `oneOf` is one-hot — exactly one, which is
  *not* XOR (parity would also fire on the all-set corner). Relations between types are **judgments
  computed from the formulas**, never authored: subtype is implication (`integer` below `number`,
  everything below `true`), equivalence is mutual implication (empty object ≡ empty array),
  disjointness an unsatisfiable meet, overlap a satisfiable meet between incomparable types — omni
  nodes are where a value-test and an element-test meet.
- >
  On this algebra sits the matching stance ([what it doesn't test, it
  tolerates](::language:principles:tolerance)): the spec mandates neither open nor closed matching;
  each consumer carries its own formula. A matcher inspects a node's **type facets** — four
  observations:
- !!<format: text/plain> |
  value:    the self-value's scalar type — or none
  format:   the value-format (text/markdown, application/pdf)
            or the shape-format (x-yamlover-chapter)
  keyed:    at least one named element exists
  ordinal:  at least one positional element exists
- >
  A tolerant matcher tests only what it needs — a markdown renderer tests `format = text/markdown` and
  nothing else, so a chunk that gained annotation keys still matches; a strict consumer opts into a
  closed shape ("a bare string, no fields") by testing every facet. Among the consumers a node
  satisfies, **specificity** picks the winner: a stricter formula outranks a looser one.
- >
  A worked failure this design removes: a markdown chunk gains `yamlover-annotations`. Facet-wise it
  is now a string-valued node with keyed elements. A registry keyed on the exact kind-and-format pair
  misses it; the tolerant formula — format only — still claims it, and the annotation layer reads the
  keyed facet separately.
